WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
I really struggled with how to cover  this story of a group of Magha  hat-wearing Trump supporting high school  students from Covington Kentucky who  surrounded and mocked a Native American  military veteran at a Washington DC  rally because at first I saw posts on  Twitter and Facebook about how the only  perpetrators here were the entitled  smirking privileged mostly white  anti-abortion Catholic school kids and  how it was wrong that they surrounded  this Native American protester in this  intimidating way I also started seeing  posts with a very different perspective  which was the kids did nothing wrong the  problem was that there was instigation  not only by Nathan Phillips the Native  American military veteran who was  singing and playing a drum but also by  so-called black Muslims who were not  actually black Muslims and we'll get to  that a little bit later and that  actually these kids should be applauded  for not becoming violent when they were  approached by a very hostile crowd the  truth is neither of those extremes and  we are now fortunately getting some more  nuance on the story and I know that  probably everyone will be upset with me  for my understanding of the story but as  I've always done on the program I'm not  going to give you anything other than my  honest and genuine perspective so let's  get into it  this all happened after an indigenous  peoples march and an anti-abortion march  both in Washington DC sort of collided  okay  and I could focus on the core singing of  the culture under Trump I could talk  about education as the catalyst here or  violence we could make it about racism  and xenophobia we could make it about  Catholic schools and parenting and  social class and entitlement and it  would be a story about all of those  things but this one actually mostly is a  story about toxic partisan politics  combined with elements of all of that  other stuff this is the last show that  would be caught doing the both sides are  to blame thing when that's not actually  the case okay when it comes to take your  pick fake news it's not both sides the  right is overwhelming  the perpetrator and perpetuator of fake  news when it comes to voter suppression  it's not both sides it's the right that  has figured out that they benefit from  fewer people voting and it's mostly  almost exclusively the right it's trying  to hold down the boat but I went back  and I took a look at this nearly  two-hour video of the incident in  Washington DC and there is no shortage  of blame to go around here for what went  on and what's really important here is  that one person or groups wrongs don't  justify anyone elses wrongs here this is  not a defense of these maggot wearing  kids and we'll get to that but the point  is the story that the only thing to  comment on here is these maggot at  wearing kids who were standing around a  Native American protester is simply  wrong and even the New York Times which  is no bastion of right-wing conspiracy  theories is saying that the full video  part of which we're playing now for  people tells a very different story than  just that initial piece of the video  that went viral the initial piece that  went viral was a Native American  military veteran Nathan Phillips beating  a drum and singing surrounded by a group  of young mostly white teenagers that  looked like they came not from an  anti-abortion rally but from a Trump  rally where they spent all of their  allowance money on Trump apparel and  hats right and in fact in the video you  see at one point that one of the kids is  standing about 12 inches from Nathan  Phillips is faced with this ludicrous  smirk that was at first the only part of  the video that got attention and it  should have gotten attention because  these kids are standing around one guy  in a way that looks intimidating they're  entitled smirks should piss anyone off  it certainly pissed me off their school  is investigating what took place the  school says they'll take appropriate  action these are kids who also are  pictured in other stories that have come  out about them at a basketball game for  example against a mostly black team  screaming at the black players some of  them had painted their entire bodies  basically instead of blackface it was  like a black body there's every  indication that these kids are  extraordinarily annoying entitled  ignorant clowns enabled by their parents  teachers chaperones whoever else's  around them that is a story here but to  pretend that they simply showed up  looking for trouble while no one else  was misbehaving and there were no other  factors here would be wrong and I'm not  going to do that first of all there are  numerous disputes about how they even  ended up in the same spot as the Native  American protester here  there were also pro-choice people who  were not behaving perfectly here there  are reports that indigenous peoples  protesters were yelling go back to  Europe to these white kids that's  actually on video that's insane and that  needs to be denounced there was a group  of they were called by some people black  Muslims but they're not they're black  Israelites who were proselytizing in the  very very inflammatory way that they do  yelling at everybody including at these  kids they if you're not familiar with  the black Israelites they're a group of  black Americans who claims that they are  the true descendants of the Israelites  and sort of in a sense the real Jews  although there's sort of a crossover  group between being Jews and Christians  but really what they're known for is  being inflammatory Street Preachers  which they chose to do at a central  point between two conflicting political  rallies reports include that the black  Israelites started arguing with the magg  of kids they were reportedly calling  them child molested [ __ ] dirty ass  crackers demon animals future school  shooters and racist bastards and at a  certain point in all of this Nathan  Phillips shows up when the black  Israelites are taunting the kids and he  walks into the crowd of students  choosing to do this he could have opted  to walk in a different way from the  video that I saw Nathan Phillips walked  to the kids and the kids just stood  there there are people reporting that  there were chants of build the wall  against the indigenous peoples that's  disgusting and that of course proves  that the border wall has never actually  been about border security because if it  were you wouldn't be yelling that at  Native Americans it's about xenophobia  and racism and it's vile but the problem  I have with this Pat is that it became  an Internet mob with people cherry  picking out of a two-hour video trying  to identify some of these underage kids  who of course bear significant  responsibility themselves but also our  kids they're the product of their  parents where were the chaperones why  did the school allow them to go to an  anti-choice rally dress like they're  coming from a Trump rally  so I'm not here to rank who did the  worst thing or to say who's more to  blame and I'm not saying that everyone  is equally to blame but what's clear to  me is that I can't think of anyone who  behaved particularly appropriately here  and I am with you and being disgusted  with the magic id's  I am with you in being disgusted by  everything we're learning about things  that have taken place at basketball  games and their behavior but we can't  pretend Pat that they were the unique  and sole instigators here and there were  certainly a bunch of misunderstandings  when you take a look at even that entire  two-hour clip you may not come away  knowing exactly what happened because  it's just not the best video quality not  the best audio quality one thing I will  point out though is it's unclear that  nathan phillips went when he walked up  to those those group of kids like i  don't know if they necessarily thought  that he was being combative or what he  was trying to do because they responded  like it was some sort of pep rally like  it was like maybe nathan phillips was  like playing along with them i don't  know i think you have to kind of give  the kids at least and out on that one  because i don't know if they realize if  they were being offensive or if the  situation was going to escalate but  certainly the responsibility does not  bear solely on them i know that there  are people on the Left who are going to  be mad at me how dare i questioned the  initial narrative how dare I question  the narrative that places blame only on  the kids I think that these kids are  quite frankly and the more we learn  about other things that have happened  involving their school it's absolutely  horrible but we we need to be full in  our reporting and I think in the long  run everybody loses by pushing a  narrative that's increasingly  demonstrably false based on the video if  you disagree with me I do want to hear  from you

